WEBVTT

00:00:01.271 --> 00:00:07.556
Sherlock Holmes, The Blue Diamond by Sir Arthur Conan Doyle.

00:00:07.616 --> 00:00:11.450
Text Adaptation by Bill Bowler.

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:14.732
Read by Wayne Forrester.

00:00:14.792 --> 00:00:18.685
Copyright Oxford University Press, 2002.

00:00:18.725 --> 00:00:19.106
Chapter 1.

00:00:19.146 --> 00:00:28.752
An Old Hat.

00:00:28.812 --> 00:00:41.976
My name's Dr. Watson, and I'm a good friend of the famous detective Sherlock Holmes.

00:00:42.036 --> 00:00:51.840
Two days after Christmas last year, I went to his house at 221B Baker Street.

00:00:51.899 --> 00:00:56.210
I wanted to say, Happy Christmas to him.

00:00:56.270 --> 00:01:01.636
When I arrived, I found him in the sitting room.

00:01:01.696 --> 00:01:06.982
He was by the window with some newspapers next to him.

00:01:07.042 --> 00:01:16.634
There was an old hat on a chair near him, and he had a magnifying glass in his hand.

00:01:16.694 --> 00:01:21.206
You're working on something, I said.

00:01:21.266 --> 00:01:22.576
Shall I go?

00:01:22.596 --> 00:01:26.838
No, said Holmes.

00:01:26.898 --> 00:01:33.296
Sit down and look at that interesting old hat over there.

00:01:33.355 --> 00:01:36.193
I sat down.

00:01:36.253 --> 00:01:43.977
It was cold out in the street, but it was nice and warm in Holmes' sitting-room.

00:01:44.037 --> 00:01:48.930
Why are you interested in that old hat?

00:01:48.989 --> 00:01:52.281
Is it something to do with a crime?

00:01:52.301 --> 00:01:54.292
I asked.

00:01:54.352 --> 00:01:56.474
Holmes laughed.

00:01:56.534 --> 00:01:58.825
Not a crime?

00:01:58.845 --> 00:01:59.255
No!

00:01:59.315 --> 00:02:00.786
He said.

00:02:00.846 --> 00:02:03.712
I got it from Peterson.

00:02:03.772 --> 00:02:08.408
the doorman at the Baker Street Hotel.

00:02:08.468 --> 00:02:16.153
He found it in the street and brought it here on Christmas Day for me to look at.

00:02:16.213 --> 00:02:26.378
He also brought a dead bird with him, a good fat Christmas goose at the same time.

00:02:26.438 --> 00:02:30.531
I gave the goose back to him this morning.

00:02:30.591 --> 00:02:38.136
He's cooking it at his house now and he's going to eat it for dinner tonight.

00:02:38.196 --> 00:02:46.400
Oh, first it was a hat, and now you're talking about a goose, I said.

00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:48.500
I don't understand.

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:56.114
Then let's begin when it all began, said Holmes.

00:02:56.174 --> 00:03:04.818
At about four o'clock in the morning of Christmas day, Peterson went home after work.

00:03:04.878 --> 00:03:16.036
When he got to Tottenham Court Road, he saw, in the street in front of him, a tall man with a goose over his shoulder.

00:03:16.096 --> 00:03:20.739
Peterson walked behind him for some time.

00:03:20.798 --> 00:03:26.043
There were some young men in the street in front of them.

00:03:26.102 --> 00:03:34.998
Suddenly One of them hit the tall man's hat off his head, and it fell into the road.

00:03:35.058 --> 00:03:49.432
Then the tall man tried to hit the young man with his walking stick, but by accident he broke the window of a shop behind him.

00:03:49.492 --> 00:03:55.378
At that moment Peterson ran to the man to help him.

00:03:55.438 --> 00:03:59.894
but the tall man ran away.

00:03:59.954 --> 00:04:05.106
Perhaps he felt bad about breaking the shop window.

00:04:05.166 --> 00:04:14.209
Perhaps he thought that Peterson, in his doorman's coat and hat, was a policeman.

00:04:14.268 --> 00:04:21.881
When he ran, he left his Christmas bird in the street next to his hat.

00:04:21.941 --> 00:04:36.274
The young men ran away at the same time, So Peterson took the goose and the hat home with him, and the next day he brought them here.

00:04:36.333 --> 00:04:44.868
There was an interesting little ticket on the goose's left leg, said Holmes.

00:04:44.928 --> 00:04:54.154
It said, for Mr. and Mrs. Henry Baker, we can find the letters H.B.

00:04:54.214 --> 00:04:56.756
in the hat, too.

00:04:56.816 --> 00:05:06.390
Oh, so the owner of the hat and the goose is called Henry Baker, I said.

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:09.842
Yes, answered Holmes.

00:05:09.902 --> 00:05:16.214
But my dear Watson, this doesn't help us very much.

00:05:16.274 --> 00:05:20.476
There are hundreds of Henry Bakers in London.

00:05:20.536 --> 00:05:24.228
I gave the goose back to Peterson this morning.

00:05:24.287 --> 00:05:25.680
He went on.

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:30.326
and I said to him, have this for your dinner.

00:05:30.386 --> 00:05:34.759
I didn't want it to go bad, you know."

00:05:34.819 --> 00:05:43.185
Did Mr. Baker put an advertisement in the newspaper about his hat and goose?

00:05:43.245 --> 00:05:44.566
I asked.

00:05:44.626 --> 00:05:45.347
No.

00:05:45.367 --> 00:05:47.879
I answered Holmes.

00:05:47.938 --> 00:05:51.822
Then, how can we find him?

00:05:51.881 --> 00:05:58.456
Well, Perhaps his hat can help us, said Holmes.

00:05:58.516 --> 00:06:01.998
Here's my magnifying glass, Watson.

00:06:02.058 --> 00:06:06.942
Now, you'll be a detective for a minute or two.

00:06:07.002 --> 00:06:12.546
What can you tell me about the owner of this hat?

00:06:12.606 --> 00:06:18.000
I took the magnifying glass and looked at the hat.

00:06:18.059 --> 00:06:21.381
It was black but old.

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:24.290
and very, very dirty.

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:27.402
I saw the letters H.B.

00:06:27.462 --> 00:06:28.412
in it.

00:06:28.472 --> 00:06:35.066
For me, it was no different from any other old black hat.

00:06:35.126 --> 00:06:43.470
I can see nothing, I said, and I gave the hat back to my friend.

00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:46.502
Excuse me, Watson.

00:06:46.562 --> 00:06:48.578
You do see.

00:06:48.638 --> 00:06:52.844
But you don't think about what you see."

00:06:52.904 --> 00:06:56.026
All right, I said.

00:06:56.086 --> 00:06:59.468
What can you see in this hat?

00:06:59.528 --> 00:07:06.490
The owner of this hat is an intelligent man, said Holmes.

00:07:06.550 --> 00:07:11.182
He was once rich and is now poor.

00:07:11.242 --> 00:07:17.018
His wife loved him once, but she doesn't love him now.

00:07:17.078 --> 00:07:21.554
and he's 30 or 40 years old.

00:07:21.614 --> 00:07:28.976
Well, perhaps I'm slow homes, but I don't understand, I said.

00:07:29.036 --> 00:07:33.618
Why is he an intelligent man, do you think?

00:07:33.677 --> 00:07:37.568
Homes put the hat on his head.

00:07:37.628 --> 00:07:41.560
It came down to his nose.

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:45.352
This is a big hat.

00:07:45.412 --> 00:07:49.868
A man with a big hat has a big head.

00:07:49.928 --> 00:07:54.120
And a man with a big head has a big brain.

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:59.472
A man with a big brain thinks a lot.

00:07:59.532 --> 00:08:05.145
But you say he was once rich and is now poor.

00:08:05.205 --> 00:08:06.866
Why?

00:08:06.926 --> 00:08:10.597
The hat is three years old.

00:08:10.657 --> 00:08:14.339
I remember these hats were in all the shops then.

00:08:14.399 --> 00:08:17.982
They were very expensive too.

00:08:18.042 --> 00:08:31.832
Three years ago this man bought a good hat, so he was rich then, but he has no money to buy a new hat now, so these days he is poor.

00:08:31.892 --> 00:08:39.025
All right, I said, but you say he is in his thirties or forties.

00:08:39.086 --> 00:08:42.047
How does the hat tell you this?

00:08:42.107 --> 00:08:51.552
Well, when I looked carefully at the hat with my magnifying glass, I could see some grey hairs in it.

00:08:51.612 --> 00:08:57.334
People usually get grey hair in their thirties or forties.

00:08:57.394 --> 00:08:58.844
I see.

00:08:58.904 --> 00:09:01.144
But what about his wife?

00:09:01.204 --> 00:09:04.185
You say she doesn't love him.

00:09:04.245 --> 00:09:07.626
Because the hat is very dirty.

00:09:07.685 --> 00:09:13.486
When a woman loves her husband, she cleans his hat for him.

00:09:13.546 --> 00:09:16.654
Perhaps he hasn't got a wife.

00:09:16.714 --> 00:09:18.724
Yes, he has.

00:09:18.784 --> 00:09:22.017
Remember the ticket on the goose's leg?

00:09:22.076 --> 00:09:24.819
Ah, yes, I said.

00:09:24.879 --> 00:09:28.372
You have an answer for everything.

00:09:28.432 --> 00:09:36.209
At that moment, the door opened and Peterson, the hotel doorman, ran into the room.

00:09:36.229 --> 00:09:39.522
He looked very excited.

00:09:39.582 --> 00:09:44.606
The goose, Mr. Holmes, the goose, he said.

00:09:44.666 --> 00:09:47.358
What's the matter with a goose?"

00:09:47.418 --> 00:09:48.948
asked Holmes.

00:09:49.008 --> 00:09:53.782
Did it come back from the dead and fly off through the kitchen window?

00:09:53.802 --> 00:09:56.994
No, Mr. Holmes!

00:09:57.054 --> 00:10:01.116
My wife found this in the bird!

00:10:01.176 --> 00:10:04.489
Peterson opened his hand.

00:10:04.549 --> 00:10:12.610
There was a beautiful blue diamond in it.

00:10:12.670 --> 00:10:31.840
Chapter 2 The Blue Diamond When Sherlock Holmes saw the diamond in the Dormans' hand, he sat up.

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:36.291
Well, well, Peterson, he said.

00:10:36.351 --> 00:10:40.702
What a wonderful thing to find in a goose.

00:10:40.762 --> 00:10:44.748
Do you know what you have in your hand?

00:10:44.808 --> 00:10:48.746
I think it's a diamond, Mr. Holmes.

00:10:48.805 --> 00:10:50.978
Is it expensive?

00:10:51.038 --> 00:10:55.312
Yes, it is, said Holmes.

00:10:55.372 --> 00:11:00.456
This is the Countess of Morker's blue diamond.

00:11:00.516 --> 00:11:03.298
It disappeared a week ago.

00:11:03.358 --> 00:11:05.600
How do you know that?

00:11:05.660 --> 00:11:07.072
I said.

00:11:07.132 --> 00:11:16.098
Watson, you must read the newspapers more often There's an advertisement in the Times today about it.

00:11:16.158 --> 00:11:18.400
Here, look!

00:11:18.460 --> 00:11:21.870
Holmes gave the advertisement to me.

00:11:21.930 --> 00:11:25.152
Then Holmes spoke to the doorman.

00:11:25.212 --> 00:11:28.924
It's a very expensive jewel, Peterson.

00:11:28.984 --> 00:11:33.536
The Countess paid about 20,000 pounds for it.

00:11:33.596 --> 00:11:40.299
Last week, someone took it from her rooms in the Cosmopolitan Hotel.

00:11:40.359 --> 00:11:44.801
Now the Countess wants to get her diamond back.

00:11:44.861 --> 00:11:50.722
She says that she's going to give a thousand pounds to the finder.

00:11:50.782 --> 00:11:53.644
A thousand pounds?

00:11:53.704 --> 00:11:56.154
cried Peterson excitedly.

00:11:56.214 --> 00:12:02.496
Then, without saying more, he sat down in the chair between us.

00:12:02.556 --> 00:12:07.781
First he looked at Holmes, and then he looked at me.

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:14.178
The diamond disappeared five days ago, I think, I said.

00:12:14.238 --> 00:12:17.882
Yes, answered Holmes.

00:12:17.942 --> 00:12:23.176
They say a young man, John Horner, took it.

00:12:23.236 --> 00:12:27.180
Here's a newspaper report about the case.

00:12:27.240 --> 00:12:33.514
Holmes gave an old newspaper to me, and I read the report.

00:12:33.574 --> 00:12:39.517
A diamond disappears from the Cosmopolitan Hotel.

00:12:39.578 --> 00:12:51.348
The counters of Morker's blue diamond disappeared from her room at the Cosmopolitan Hotel on the 22nd of this month.

00:12:51.408 --> 00:13:03.141
The police, think John Horner, 26 years old, took the diamond from a jewel box when he went to repair the window in the room.

00:13:03.201 --> 00:13:17.178
James Ryder, the assistant manager of the hotel, told the court, I took Horner to the Countess's room, but then I went away for some time.

00:13:17.238 --> 00:13:28.764
When I came back, Horner wasn't there, and I found the open box, without the jewel in it, on the table next to the bed.

00:13:28.824 --> 00:13:35.491
A Catherine Cusack, the Countess is made, spoke next.

00:13:35.511 --> 00:13:40.694
I heard Mr. Ryder call, and I ran to the Countess's room.

00:13:40.754 --> 00:13:46.258
There I found Ryder with the jewel box in front of him.

00:13:46.318 --> 00:13:54.172
The police found Horner at his home later that day, but they couldn't find the diamond.

00:13:54.232 --> 00:13:59.360
Later, Detective Bradstreet spoke to the court.

00:13:59.421 --> 00:14:04.891
When I said you're a diamond thief, Horner hit me.

00:14:04.951 --> 00:14:11.406
Soon after that, Horner told the court angrily, you've got the wrong man.

00:14:11.426 --> 00:14:15.380
I didn't take the Countess's diamond.

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:17.412
I'm not a thief.

00:14:17.472 --> 00:14:25.488
Bradstreet then told the court, Horner went to prison once before for being a thief.

00:14:25.548 --> 00:14:28.310
I say he took the diamond.

00:14:28.370 --> 00:14:33.062
The case goes to the High Court next week.

00:14:33.123 --> 00:14:40.106
Well, that's the newspaper report about the diamond, said Holmes.

00:14:40.166 --> 00:14:51.922
Now we need to understand how the jewel left the Countess of Morker's room in the Cosmopolitan Hotel and arrived in Tottenham Court Road in a goose.

00:14:51.982 --> 00:14:57.142
You see, Watson, there is a crime in this case.

00:14:57.202 --> 00:14:59.548
Here's the diamond.

00:14:59.608 --> 00:15:02.630
The diamond came from the goose.

00:15:02.690 --> 00:15:09.542
And the goose came from Mr. Henry Baker, the man with the old hat.

00:15:09.602 --> 00:15:17.764
I know you felt a little bored when I told you all about Mr. Baker, but now we must find him.

00:15:17.824 --> 00:15:24.874
Where and how does he come into the case of the blue diamond?

00:15:24.934 --> 00:15:30.415
The answer to these two questions is most important.

00:15:30.475 --> 00:15:33.538
But how can we find him?

00:15:33.598 --> 00:15:35.298
I asked.

00:15:35.358 --> 00:15:40.874
Through a newspaper advertisement answered Holmes.

00:15:40.934 --> 00:15:45.297
He took a pen and began to write.

00:15:45.357 --> 00:15:53.480
Did you lose a goose and a black hat some days ago in Tottenham Court Road?

00:15:53.540 --> 00:15:56.909
Are you called Henry Baker?

00:15:56.969 --> 00:16:06.912
Please come to 221B Baker Street this evening at 630 to get your things.

00:16:06.972 --> 00:16:08.882
There.

00:16:08.942 --> 00:16:12.724
That says it all, I think, said Holmes.

00:16:12.784 --> 00:16:14.234
Yes.

00:16:14.294 --> 00:16:16.205
But is he going to read it?

00:16:16.265 --> 00:16:17.755
I asked.

00:16:17.815 --> 00:16:19.596
Yes, Watson.

00:16:19.656 --> 00:16:21.386
I think he is.

00:16:21.446 --> 00:16:24.344
He's a poor man, remember?

00:16:24.404 --> 00:16:38.007
At the time he was afraid because of his accident with the shop window, but now I believe he feels very sorry about running away and thinks, why did I leave that bird in the street?

00:16:38.066 --> 00:16:43.589
Perhaps he looked for a newspaper advertisement about it yesterday.

00:16:43.649 --> 00:16:47.891
I think he's going to look again today too.

00:16:47.951 --> 00:16:52.160
What's more, we've got his name in the advertisement.

00:16:52.220 --> 00:16:57.459
So I believe his friends are going to see it, and tell him about it.

00:16:57.520 --> 00:17:01.752
Yes, I see, I said.

00:17:01.812 --> 00:17:15.228
Holmes gave some money to the doorman and said, Peterson, please go down to the newspaper offices and put this advertisement in all the evening newspapers.

00:17:15.287 --> 00:17:17.408
Very good, sir.

00:17:17.468 --> 00:17:21.450
And shall I leave the diamond with you, sir?

00:17:21.511 --> 00:17:23.711
Yes, Peterson.

00:17:23.772 --> 00:17:33.976
And I say, Peterson, after you leave the newspaper offices, can you buy a goose and bring it back here?

00:17:34.036 --> 00:17:38.688
We need a new bird for Mr. Henry Baker when he comes.

00:17:38.748 --> 00:17:45.050
Your family is eating the old one for dinner today, so he can't have that.

00:17:45.110 --> 00:17:52.734
The doorman went out of the door and down the street.

00:17:52.794 --> 00:18:09.687
Chapter 3 Mr. Henry Baker Holmes took the blue diamond in his hand and looked at it.

00:18:09.747 --> 00:18:13.750
What a beautiful thing, he said.

00:18:13.810 --> 00:18:16.682
Look at the wonderful colors in it.

00:18:16.742 --> 00:18:20.525
Dark blues and cold whites.

00:18:20.585 --> 00:18:26.218
All big jewels make people into thieves and killers in the end.

00:18:26.278 --> 00:18:32.761
This one comes from the south of China, near the Amoi River.

00:18:32.822 --> 00:18:37.704
It's only 20 years old, so it's a young thing.

00:18:37.764 --> 00:18:43.628
But already, many terrible crimes are happening because of it.

00:18:43.688 --> 00:18:57.052
I'm going to put it in my safe now, And then let's write a letter to the Countess of Morka and say we have her beautiful blue diamond here with us.

00:18:57.112 --> 00:19:02.314
But Holmes, I said, I don't understand.

00:19:02.374 --> 00:19:06.305
Is that young man-horner innocent after all?

00:19:06.325 --> 00:19:10.176
I don't know.

00:19:10.236 --> 00:19:13.467
And what about Henry Baker?

00:19:13.527 --> 00:19:17.378
The tall man with the hat and the goose.

00:19:17.438 --> 00:19:21.479
Is he the jewel thief, perhaps?

00:19:21.539 --> 00:19:23.169
No.

00:19:23.229 --> 00:19:25.650
I don't think he is.

00:19:25.710 --> 00:19:28.411
I believe he's an innocent man.

00:19:28.431 --> 00:19:34.652
I don't think he knew there was an expensive diamond in his goose.

00:19:34.712 --> 00:19:39.353
A jewel worth more than 20,000 pounds.

00:19:39.413 --> 00:19:42.074
But let's wait and see.

00:19:42.134 --> 00:19:51.390
Perhaps Mr. Baker's going to answer our advertisement this evening and then we can learn something more about him.

00:19:51.450 --> 00:19:57.504
All right, I said, I can come back after work this evening.

00:19:57.564 --> 00:20:01.817
I'm very interested in the answer to this case.

00:20:01.878 --> 00:20:04.670
Good, replied Holmes.

00:20:04.730 --> 00:20:07.922
Dinner is at seven o'clock.

00:20:07.982 --> 00:20:13.170
I got to Baker Street at six thirty that evening.

00:20:13.231 --> 00:20:21.491
There was a tall man already at Sherlock Holmes's front door when I came down the street.

00:20:21.552 --> 00:20:28.706
He wore a long winter coat and had a Scottish hat on his head.

00:20:28.766 --> 00:20:33.700
When I arrived next to him, the door opened.

00:20:33.760 --> 00:20:40.346
Mrs. Hudson, Holmes's housekeeper, said, Good evening to the two of us.

00:20:40.406 --> 00:20:45.592
and we went in and upstairs to Holmes' room.

00:20:45.652 --> 00:20:53.748
Mr. Henry Baker, I believe, said Holmes to the man when he came in.

00:20:53.808 --> 00:20:56.960
Please, sit down.

00:20:57.020 --> 00:21:00.922
Holmes looked at me and smiled.

00:21:00.982 --> 00:21:02.994
Ah, Watson!

00:21:03.054 --> 00:21:04.115
Good!

00:21:04.175 --> 00:21:06.738
You are here when we need you.

00:21:06.798 --> 00:21:11.456
Then he looked back at his other visitor.

00:21:11.516 --> 00:21:15.252
Is that your hat, Mr. Baker?

00:21:15.312 --> 00:21:19.814
Mr. Baker looked at the hat on the chair.

00:21:19.874 --> 00:21:22.474
Yes, sir.

00:21:22.534 --> 00:21:24.704
That's my hat.

00:21:24.764 --> 00:21:27.755
There is no question about it.

00:21:27.815 --> 00:21:36.156
He was a big man with a big head, an intelligent face, and grey hair.

00:21:36.216 --> 00:21:39.640
I remembered Holmes's words about him.

00:21:39.700 --> 00:21:51.478
He wore a dirty old black coat with no shirt under it, but he spoke slowly, quietly and carefully.

00:21:51.538 --> 00:22:00.724
I looked at him and listened to him, and I thought, yes, this is an intelligent man.

00:22:00.784 --> 00:22:08.742
He was rich once, but now he has no money, and things aren't easy for him.

00:22:08.802 --> 00:22:15.310
We found your hat and your goose some days ago, said Holmes.

00:22:15.370 --> 00:22:19.764
But we couldn't find you very easily, Mr. Baker.

00:22:19.824 --> 00:22:22.706
We didn't know your address.

00:22:22.766 --> 00:22:28.070
Why didn't you put an advertisement in the newspaper with your address in it?

00:22:28.130 --> 00:22:35.025
We waited and waited for an advertisement from you, but saw nothing.

00:22:35.085 --> 00:22:39.170
Mr. Baker smiled.

00:22:39.230 --> 00:22:41.463
I'm sorry.

00:22:41.523 --> 00:22:48.258
Advertisements are expensive, and I haven't got a lot of money these days.

00:22:48.318 --> 00:22:51.780
I had once, but not now.

00:22:51.840 --> 00:22:53.322
He went on.

00:22:53.382 --> 00:23:08.829
And, well, I thought those young men in Tottenham Court Road had my hat and my goose, and I didn't want to put an expensive advertisement in the newspaper for nothing.

00:23:08.889 --> 00:23:12.777
I understand," said Holmes.

00:23:12.837 --> 00:23:20.238
Now, before we say more, I must tell you something about your goose, Mr. Baker.

00:23:20.298 --> 00:23:26.980
I'm sorry, but, well, we ate it yesterday, you know.

00:23:27.040 --> 00:23:29.230
You ate it!

00:23:29.290 --> 00:23:33.621
said our visitor, and he stood up excitedly.

00:23:33.681 --> 00:23:39.269
Yes, well, we didn't want it to go bad, you see.

00:23:39.329 --> 00:23:43.767
But we bought a nice new goose this morning for you.

00:23:43.827 --> 00:23:46.988
It's on the table there, by the door.

00:23:47.048 --> 00:23:49.339
Is that all right for you?"

00:23:49.399 --> 00:23:50.549
Oh!

00:23:50.609 --> 00:23:51.870
Yes!

00:23:51.930 --> 00:23:53.470
Yes!"

00:23:53.530 --> 00:23:55.861
said Mr. Baker happily.

00:23:55.921 --> 00:23:58.172
He sat down again.

00:23:58.232 --> 00:24:01.122
And let's see.

00:24:01.183 --> 00:24:09.220
I think we have your old goose's feet, head, and everything from inside it in the kitchen.

00:24:09.280 --> 00:24:11.528
Do you want those?"

00:24:11.588 --> 00:24:13.818
The man laughed.

00:24:13.878 --> 00:24:15.119
No!

00:24:15.179 --> 00:24:16.250
No!

00:24:16.310 --> 00:24:17.360
He said.

00:24:17.421 --> 00:24:22.043
But I'd like to take that nice new goose home with me.

00:24:22.103 --> 00:24:24.605
Thank you very much.

00:24:24.665 --> 00:24:29.617
Sherlock Holmes looked at me with a little smile.

00:24:29.678 --> 00:24:33.580
Very well, he said to Mr. Baker.

00:24:33.640 --> 00:24:37.292
There is your hat, and there is your bird.

00:24:37.352 --> 00:24:38.954
Please take them.

00:24:39.014 --> 00:24:44.478
Oh, and before you go, can you tell me something?

00:24:44.538 --> 00:24:47.309
Where did you get your goose?

00:24:47.369 --> 00:24:53.922
I know a lot about geese, and that was a very good bird, I can tell you.

00:24:53.982 --> 00:24:57.693
Well, sir, said Baker.

00:24:57.753 --> 00:25:02.864
He stood up and took his hat and the goose in his hands.

00:25:02.924 --> 00:25:06.660
I got that bird at the alpha.

00:25:06.720 --> 00:25:09.886
A pub near the British Museum.

00:25:09.946 --> 00:25:16.460
This year, the owner of the pub, Mr. Windigate, began a goose club.

00:25:16.520 --> 00:25:27.018
Every week, we all put five or six pence into a money box, and at Christmas time, we all had the money for a goose.

00:25:27.078 --> 00:25:32.212
With that, he said goodbye and left.

00:25:32.272 --> 00:25:36.534
Well, said my detective friend.

00:25:36.594 --> 00:25:38.983
That answers one question.

00:25:39.044 --> 00:25:42.796
Mr. Baker is not our diamond thief.

00:25:42.856 --> 00:25:46.300
Are you hungry, Watson?

00:25:46.360 --> 00:25:47.991
No, not very.

00:25:48.052 --> 00:25:49.923
Let's eat later then.

00:25:49.983 --> 00:25:52.736
We must go to the Alpha at once.

00:25:52.796 --> 00:25:58.980
We need to speak to Mr. Windigate tonight.

00:25:59.040 --> 00:26:17.122
Chapter 4 To Mr. Breckenridge's Holmes and I put on our coats and hats and went out into the cold winter street.

00:26:17.183 --> 00:26:20.444
The sky was dark over our heads.

00:26:20.504 --> 00:26:27.160
We walked east, and in a quarter of an hour we stood in front of the alpha.

00:26:27.220 --> 00:26:32.103
Holmes opened the door and we went in.

00:26:32.163 --> 00:26:39.277
In the pub the owner, Mr. Windigate, gave us some beer.

00:26:39.337 --> 00:26:41.907
Is this be a good?"

00:26:41.967 --> 00:26:43.988
Holmes asked him.

00:26:44.048 --> 00:26:48.269
I asked because I know your geese are very good.

00:26:48.329 --> 00:26:53.820
Mr. Henry Baker told us all about your goose-club.

00:26:53.880 --> 00:26:56.090
Oh, yes.

00:26:56.150 --> 00:26:59.431
But those geese weren't our geese.

00:26:59.491 --> 00:27:04.502
They came from a man with a little shop in Covent Garden.

00:27:04.562 --> 00:27:07.453
Breaking Ridge is his name.

00:27:07.513 --> 00:27:08.940
Thank you.

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:11.978
My good man, the said Holmes.

00:27:12.038 --> 00:27:15.820
We paid for our beer and drank it.

00:27:15.880 --> 00:27:22.064
Then we walked out of the warm pub and into the cold night again.

00:27:22.124 --> 00:27:26.405
Now for Covent Garden, the said Holmes.

00:27:26.466 --> 00:27:30.978
And we walked down the street past the British Museum.

00:27:31.038 --> 00:27:33.029
Remember Watson?

00:27:33.089 --> 00:27:35.468
It all began with a goose.

00:27:35.529 --> 00:27:41.202
but it finishes with seven years in prison for young Mr. Horner.

00:27:41.262 --> 00:27:48.387
Perhaps we can learn more about this interesting case in Mr. Breckinridge's shop.

00:27:48.447 --> 00:27:54.392
We walked south and soon came to Mr. Breckinridge's shop.

00:27:54.452 --> 00:27:58.716
A Breckinridge and a boy were at the door.

00:27:58.776 --> 00:28:02.548
It was nearly time to close for the night.

00:28:02.608 --> 00:28:04.525
Good evening.

00:28:04.585 --> 00:28:07.681
"'It's a cold night,' said Holmes.

00:28:07.701 --> 00:28:11.012
"'How can I help you?'

00:28:11.072 --> 00:28:13.254
asked Breaking Ridge.

00:28:13.314 --> 00:28:16.385
Holmes looked at the empty shop window.

00:28:16.405 --> 00:28:20.447
"'No geese, I see,' he said.

00:28:20.467 --> 00:28:25.109
"'There are some in that other shop there behind you.

00:28:25.129 --> 00:28:32.230
"'Ah, but I came to you because I hear your geese are very good.

00:28:32.290 --> 00:28:36.930
A Breckenridge's birds are the best, he said.

00:28:36.990 --> 00:28:39.052
Who said that?

00:28:39.113 --> 00:28:42.094
The owner of the alpha.

00:28:42.154 --> 00:28:44.106
Ah, yes.

00:28:44.166 --> 00:28:49.100
He had twenty-four of my geese two days before Christmas.

00:28:49.160 --> 00:28:52.404
They were very good birds too.

00:28:52.464 --> 00:28:54.484
Where did you get them?

00:28:54.544 --> 00:28:59.842
I'm not going to tell you, said Breckenridge angrily.

00:28:59.902 --> 00:29:06.980
Again and again, people come and talk to me about those geese, and I don't like it.

00:29:07.040 --> 00:29:09.302
I paid good money for them.

00:29:09.362 --> 00:29:13.645
I took them to the alpha, and then I forgot all about them.

00:29:13.706 --> 00:29:16.918
And then all the questions began.

00:29:16.978 --> 00:29:18.910
Where are the geese?

00:29:18.970 --> 00:29:21.362
How much do you want for them?

00:29:21.422 --> 00:29:23.924
Who did you sell them to?

00:29:23.984 --> 00:29:26.276
Why are people interested in them?

00:29:26.336 --> 00:29:27.607
I don't know.

00:29:27.667 --> 00:29:30.814
They aren't the only geese in London, you know.

00:29:30.874 --> 00:29:34.070
I know," said Holmes.

00:29:34.130 --> 00:29:38.032
But who asked you all those questions before?

00:29:38.092 --> 00:29:39.602
Not me.

00:29:39.662 --> 00:29:42.654
I had nothing to do with that, you know.

00:29:42.714 --> 00:29:45.696
But now I need your help.

00:29:45.756 --> 00:29:51.729
We ate a goose at the alpha, and I say it was a country goose.

00:29:51.789 --> 00:29:58.002
But my good friend, Dr. Watson, here, says it was a London goose.

00:29:58.062 --> 00:29:59.954
Which of us is right?

00:30:00.014 --> 00:30:02.176
It's an important question.

00:30:02.236 --> 00:30:05.218
Five pounds goes to the winner.

00:30:05.278 --> 00:30:12.572
Well then, you lose, and your friend is the winner, said Breckenridge.

00:30:12.632 --> 00:30:15.654
That goose came from London.

00:30:15.714 --> 00:30:20.236
I can't believe that, said Holmes.

00:30:20.296 --> 00:30:23.118
A pound says I'm right.

00:30:23.178 --> 00:30:28.490
Very well, said Holmes, and he took out a pound.

00:30:28.550 --> 00:30:33.892
I'm ready to pay, but I know you're going to lose your money."

00:30:33.952 --> 00:30:36.794
Breckinridge laughed.

00:30:36.854 --> 00:30:40.296
Breckin' me the books, Bill," he said.

00:30:40.356 --> 00:30:43.998
The boy brought two books to him.

00:30:44.058 --> 00:30:47.940
Breckinridge opened the little one.

00:30:48.000 --> 00:30:51.884
This is my address book, he said.

00:30:51.944 --> 00:30:57.446
When people sell their geese to me, their addresses go in here.

00:30:57.506 --> 00:31:02.808
country people on the left and town people on the right.

00:31:02.868 --> 00:31:08.122
The numbers, after every name, are page numbers in my big book.

00:31:08.182 --> 00:31:13.264
Read out the third name on the right, said Breaking Ridge.

00:31:13.324 --> 00:31:28.694
Mrs. Oakshot 117 Brixton Road Number 249 Red Homes Then, Breckinridge opened the big book.

00:31:28.754 --> 00:31:34.486
And this is my in and out book, he said.

00:31:34.546 --> 00:31:38.627
Let's look at page 249.

00:31:38.687 --> 00:31:40.567
Here we are.

00:31:40.628 --> 00:31:43.128
Mrs. Oakshot.

00:31:43.188 --> 00:31:45.839
What can you see for December the 22nd?

00:31:45.879 --> 00:31:59.402
24 geese from Mrs. O, Redhomes, all twenty-four to Mr. Windigate at the Alpha.

00:31:59.462 --> 00:32:01.102
There!

00:32:01.163 --> 00:32:03.164
What do you say now?

00:32:03.224 --> 00:32:05.276
The said Breckingridge.

00:32:05.336 --> 00:32:13.082
Holmes put his pound into Breckingridge's hand angrily.

00:32:13.143 --> 00:32:22.500
Chapter Five A Week, Little Man.

00:32:22.560 --> 00:32:28.312
in the street, Holmes stopped.

00:32:28.372 --> 00:32:31.413
Suddenly he wasn't angry.

00:32:31.473 --> 00:32:34.014
He began to laugh.

00:32:34.074 --> 00:32:36.524
You see, Watson?

00:32:36.584 --> 00:32:37.974
He said.

00:32:38.034 --> 00:32:43.826
Breckingridge didn't want to tell me Mrs. Oakshot's name and address at first.

00:32:43.886 --> 00:32:51.300
But later, when he saw he could easily get a pound from me, he told me everything.

00:32:51.360 --> 00:32:56.214
And he said something very interesting when he got angry.

00:32:56.274 --> 00:32:58.055
Did you hear?

00:32:58.115 --> 00:33:03.018
Other people are asking questions about those geese.

00:33:03.078 --> 00:33:10.051
Suddenly, there was a lot of noise from Mr. Breckingridge's shop behind us.

00:33:10.111 --> 00:33:12.622
We looked back at it.

00:33:12.683 --> 00:33:19.084
Breckingridge stood tall and angry in front of his shop door.

00:33:19.144 --> 00:33:24.627
A weak little man stood in front of him in the street.

00:33:24.687 --> 00:33:26.608
Look, you!

00:33:26.668 --> 00:33:28.630
Breaking Ridge shouted.

00:33:28.691 --> 00:33:32.544
I don't want to hear any more about those geese.

00:33:32.604 --> 00:33:36.607
Mrs. Oakshot can come and speak to me when she wants.

00:33:36.667 --> 00:33:38.148
But not you.

00:33:38.208 --> 00:33:40.980
You have nothing to do with it.

00:33:41.040 --> 00:33:43.602
Did I get the geese from you?

00:33:43.663 --> 00:33:44.684
No.

00:33:44.704 --> 00:33:48.836
But when was my goose, I tell you?

00:33:48.896 --> 00:33:50.806
cried the little man.

00:33:50.866 --> 00:33:54.016
Then asked Mrs. Oakshot for it.

00:33:54.076 --> 00:33:59.868
But she told me, asked Mr. Breckingridge for it.

00:33:59.928 --> 00:34:03.329
Well, that's nothing to do with me.

00:34:03.389 --> 00:34:06.400
I don't want to hear any more from you.

00:34:06.460 --> 00:34:08.100
Do you understand?

00:34:08.160 --> 00:34:10.411
Now go away!

00:34:10.471 --> 00:34:19.349
Breckingridge closed his shop door angrily, and the little man ran off down the dark street.

00:34:19.409 --> 00:34:26.909
Perhaps we don't need to visit Mrs. Oakshot in Brixton Road after all.

00:34:26.969 --> 00:34:29.800
Said Holmes to me quietly.

00:34:29.861 --> 00:34:32.912
Let's talk to that man.

00:34:32.972 --> 00:34:36.094
Perhaps he can help us.

00:34:36.154 --> 00:34:42.819
Holmes walked quickly up behind a little man and put a hand on his shoulder.

00:34:42.879 --> 00:34:47.402
The man stopped and looked back over his shoulder at us.

00:34:47.462 --> 00:34:50.224
His face was white.

00:34:50.284 --> 00:34:52.365
Who are you?

00:34:52.425 --> 00:34:54.306
What do you want?

00:34:54.366 --> 00:34:56.726
He asked weekly.

00:34:56.787 --> 00:35:00.388
Excuse me, said Holmes.

00:35:00.448 --> 00:35:04.288
But I heard your questions to that shop owner.

00:35:04.348 --> 00:35:07.590
I think I can help you.

00:35:07.650 --> 00:35:09.210
Who are you?

00:35:09.230 --> 00:35:12.731
And how can you help me?

00:35:12.791 --> 00:35:15.882
My name is Sherlock Holmes.

00:35:15.942 --> 00:35:20.699
And it's my job to know things other people don't know.

00:35:20.760 --> 00:35:24.998
But you can't know anything about this."

00:35:25.058 --> 00:35:29.299
Excuse me, I know everything.

00:35:29.360 --> 00:35:32.951
You want to find twenty-four geese.

00:35:33.011 --> 00:35:38.844
Mrs. Oakshot of Brixton Road sold them to Mr. Breckinridge here.

00:35:38.904 --> 00:35:48.982
He sold them to Mr. Windigate, the owner of the Alpha, and Mr. Windigate sold them to the people in his goose club.

00:35:49.042 --> 00:35:53.620
Oh, sir, this is wonderful.

00:35:53.680 --> 00:35:56.172
I'm very happy to meet you.

00:35:56.192 --> 00:35:59.514
You said the little man excitedly.

00:35:59.574 --> 00:36:01.306
You are right.

00:36:01.366 --> 00:36:04.987
I am very interested in those geese.

00:36:05.048 --> 00:36:07.850
More than I can say.

00:36:07.910 --> 00:36:13.163
Why don't we go to a warm room in my house for a talk then?

00:36:13.224 --> 00:36:16.769
I don't like standing here in the cold street.

00:36:16.829 --> 00:36:22.003
Holmes put out his arm, and a cab stopped for us.

00:36:22.063 --> 00:36:27.575
But before we go, can you please tell us your name?

00:36:27.635 --> 00:36:48.336
The little man looked at Holmes, and then at me before he answered, eh, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je, je We'd like to know your real name, please.

00:36:48.396 --> 00:36:53.638
The strangers face change from white to red.

00:36:53.698 --> 00:36:55.918
Very well.

00:36:55.978 --> 00:36:59.489
My real name is James Ryder.

00:36:59.549 --> 00:37:00.899
He said.

00:37:00.959 --> 00:37:03.520
Well, well, well.

00:37:03.580 --> 00:37:05.110
Said Holmes.

00:37:05.170 --> 00:37:09.601
Assistant manager of the Cosmopolitan Hotel.

00:37:09.661 --> 00:37:13.504
Now, let's get into the cab and go home.

00:37:13.564 --> 00:37:17.958
Then I can tell you everything you want to know.

00:37:18.018 --> 00:37:22.840
So, we got into the cab and went home.

00:37:22.900 --> 00:37:27.384
Ryder looked excited, but said nothing.

00:37:27.444 --> 00:37:33.117
Homes didn't speak at all in the cab.

00:37:33.177 --> 00:37:53.722
Chapter 6 One or Two Questions When we arrived at 221B Baker Street, we got out of the cab and went in.

00:37:53.782 --> 00:37:59.144
Back in his sitting room, Holmes spoke at last.

00:37:59.204 --> 00:38:02.596
Please sit down, Mr. Ryder.

00:38:02.656 --> 00:38:05.818
Now, where were we?

00:38:05.878 --> 00:38:07.818
Ah, yes.

00:38:07.878 --> 00:38:18.262
You want to know what happened to those 24 geese, or perhaps What happened to one of those geese?

00:38:18.322 --> 00:38:22.774
You are interested in only one bird, I think.

00:38:22.834 --> 00:38:26.755
A white goose with a black tail.

00:38:26.815 --> 00:38:28.956
Oh, sir!

00:38:29.016 --> 00:38:31.357
Ryder said excitedly.

00:38:31.417 --> 00:38:34.258
Where did that bird go to?

00:38:34.318 --> 00:38:36.599
It came here.

00:38:36.659 --> 00:38:39.970
And it was a most interesting bird.

00:38:40.030 --> 00:38:43.766
We found something in it after it died.

00:38:43.826 --> 00:38:47.091
The most wonderful thing.

00:38:47.151 --> 00:38:49.602
Here it is.

00:38:49.662 --> 00:38:53.464
Our visitor stood up weekly.

00:38:53.524 --> 00:38:58.686
Holmes opened his safe and took out the blue diamond.

00:38:58.746 --> 00:39:02.957
In his hand, it was cold and beautiful.

00:39:03.017 --> 00:39:07.928
Ryder looked at the jewel but said nothing.

00:39:07.989 --> 00:39:11.310
Holmes spoke for him.

00:39:11.370 --> 00:39:13.963
We know it was you, Ryder.

00:39:14.023 --> 00:39:18.178
He said, sit down and have a drink.

00:39:18.238 --> 00:39:20.568
You look very weak.

00:39:20.628 --> 00:39:23.420
I gave Ryder a drink.

00:39:23.480 --> 00:39:28.674
He sat down and drank it quickly and looked at Holmes.

00:39:28.734 --> 00:39:32.156
I saw he was afraid.

00:39:32.216 --> 00:39:37.179
You don't need to tell me much, said Holmes.

00:39:37.239 --> 00:39:44.591
I know nearly everything about the case, but I have one or two questions to ask.

00:39:44.651 --> 00:39:50.922
How did you hear of the Countess of Morka's Blue Diamond?

00:39:50.982 --> 00:39:59.505
Catherine Cusack, her maid told me, said Ryder, I see.

00:39:59.565 --> 00:40:01.666
Holmes went on.

00:40:01.726 --> 00:40:08.949
So you and Cusack wanted to get the diamond and sell it for lots of money.

00:40:09.009 --> 00:40:17.542
You asked John Horner to come and repair the window in the room because you knew of his time in prison.

00:40:17.602 --> 00:40:23.655
When he left, you took the diamond from the Countess's jewel box.

00:40:23.715 --> 00:40:26.526
Then you called the police.

00:40:26.586 --> 00:40:28.777
They came at once.

00:40:28.838 --> 00:40:34.610
Because of Horner's time in prison, they believed he was the thief.

00:40:34.670 --> 00:40:37.322
It was all very easy.

00:40:37.382 --> 00:40:42.991
Then, oh, please, please, cried Ryder.

00:40:43.052 --> 00:40:45.920
Now on the floor at Holmes' feet.

00:40:45.980 --> 00:40:48.092
Think of my father.

00:40:48.152 --> 00:40:50.083
Think of my mother.

00:40:50.143 --> 00:40:53.045
I never did anything wrong before.

00:40:53.105 --> 00:40:56.028
I'm never going to do it again.

00:40:56.088 --> 00:40:58.870
Please don't tell the police.

00:40:58.930 --> 00:41:01.632
I don't want to go to prison.

00:41:01.692 --> 00:41:06.956
Sit down in your chair, said Holmes coldly.

00:41:07.016 --> 00:41:12.810
You're crying now, but did you feel sorry for young Horner?

00:41:12.870 --> 00:41:28.728
He knew nothing of this crime, but the police believed he was a diamond thief, and so he went to court, and he is now going to prison, all because of you.

00:41:28.788 --> 00:41:37.962
I can leave the country, Mr. Holmes, then, when I don't go to court, Horner can leave prison.

00:41:38.022 --> 00:41:42.586
We can see about that later, said Holmes.

00:41:42.647 --> 00:41:47.712
But now, please tell me, Mr. Ryder.

00:41:47.772 --> 00:41:53.538
How did the diamond get from the hotel into a goose?

00:41:53.598 --> 00:41:57.712
And how did the goose get into a shop?

00:41:57.772 --> 00:42:01.676
And please tell the truth.

00:42:01.736 --> 00:42:04.958
Ryder began.

00:42:05.018 --> 00:42:14.046
When the police arrested Horner, I left the hotel with the diamond in my pocket.

00:42:14.106 --> 00:42:21.851
I didn't want to stay at the cosmopolitan, not with the police everywhere, looking at everything.

00:42:21.912 --> 00:42:27.446
So I went to my sister's house in South London.

00:42:27.506 --> 00:42:33.620
She lives in Brixton Road with her husband, Mr. Oakshot.

00:42:33.680 --> 00:42:42.812
I saw lots of police officers on my way there And when I got to Brixton Road, I was very afraid.

00:42:42.872 --> 00:42:44.532
What's the matter?

00:42:44.592 --> 00:42:46.644
My sister asked.

00:42:46.705 --> 00:42:52.428
I told her about the diamond thief and about the police arresting Horner.

00:42:52.488 --> 00:42:58.012
Then I went into the back garden to smoke and think.

00:42:58.072 --> 00:43:02.085
What could I do with the diamond now?

00:43:02.146 --> 00:43:07.053
In the garden, I remembered my friend Maudsley.

00:43:07.113 --> 00:43:14.692
He began well, but he went bad, and in the end he went to prison for his crimes.

00:43:14.752 --> 00:43:19.727
Perhaps he knows about selling diamonds, I thought.

00:43:19.787 --> 00:43:27.244
So I decided to visit him at his home in Kilburn, in North London.

00:43:27.304 --> 00:43:31.837
But how can I walk across London with the diamond?

00:43:31.897 --> 00:43:33.248
I thought.

00:43:33.308 --> 00:43:35.856
I can't have it in my pocket.

00:43:35.917 --> 00:43:39.865
not with all those police officers in the streets.

00:43:39.925 --> 00:43:47.288
Then I looked down at the geese in the garden, and I thought of something.

00:43:47.348 --> 00:43:53.491
I knew one of those geese was for me, for my Christmas dinner.

00:43:53.552 --> 00:44:00.915
So I decided to take my goose there and then, and not to wait for it.

00:44:00.975 --> 00:44:06.868
I quickly caught a big white goose with a black tail.

00:44:06.928 --> 00:44:14.402
Then I took the diamond from my pocket and put it into the bird's mouth.

00:44:14.462 --> 00:44:18.424
I felt the jewel go down its neck.

00:44:18.484 --> 00:44:23.766
With the diamond now inside the goose, I felt happy.

00:44:23.826 --> 00:44:28.278
I could walk to Kilburn and back easily.

00:44:28.338 --> 00:44:32.650
Then my sister came into the garden.

00:44:32.710 --> 00:44:36.474
What are you doing with that goose, she said?

00:44:36.534 --> 00:44:41.918
I put my bird down, and it ran off with the other geese.

00:44:41.978 --> 00:44:46.562
That's the goose I want for Christmas, I said.

00:44:46.622 --> 00:44:52.960
Very well, catch it, kill it, and take it with you, she said.

00:44:53.020 --> 00:45:00.987
Well, Mr. Holmes, I caught that bird, killed it, and took it with me to Kilbon.

00:45:01.048 --> 00:45:08.072
There I told my friend Maudsley all about the diamond, and he laughed and laughed.

00:45:08.132 --> 00:45:11.475
We got a knife and opened the goose.

00:45:11.535 --> 00:45:14.487
But we couldn't find the diamond.

00:45:14.547 --> 00:45:18.340
I knew then something was wrong.

00:45:18.400 --> 00:45:21.648
I left the goose with Maudsley.

00:45:21.708 --> 00:45:26.298
ran to my sister's house and went into the back garden.

00:45:26.358 --> 00:45:29.320
There was no geese there.

00:45:29.380 --> 00:45:31.881
Where are they all, Maggie?

00:45:31.941 --> 00:45:38.206
I said, in Mr. Breckinridge's shop, in Covent Garden.

00:45:38.266 --> 00:45:41.758
Were there two birds with black tails?

00:45:41.818 --> 00:45:43.168
I asked.

00:45:43.228 --> 00:45:47.300
Yes, there were James, she said.

00:45:47.360 --> 00:45:51.508
And to my eyes, one was no different from the other.

00:45:51.569 --> 00:45:54.205
I understood it all then.

00:45:54.265 --> 00:45:59.047
The diamond was inside the other goose, with the black tail.

00:45:59.108 --> 00:46:03.620
And that goose was now in Mr. Breckinridge's shop.

00:46:03.680 --> 00:46:09.022
I ran to Covent Garden at once, and went to Breckinridge's.

00:46:09.082 --> 00:46:12.064
But the geese weren't in his shop.

00:46:12.124 --> 00:46:18.237
And when I asked about them, he told me, I sold them all at once.

00:46:18.297 --> 00:46:20.774
But you must tell me.

00:46:20.834 --> 00:46:22.700
Where are they now?

00:46:22.760 --> 00:46:25.432
I asked again and again.

00:46:25.492 --> 00:46:27.794
But he never told me.

00:46:27.854 --> 00:46:32.717
You and Dr. Watson heard him earlier tonight, Mr. Holmes.

00:46:32.777 --> 00:46:36.550
He never answered my questions.

00:46:36.610 --> 00:46:41.394
Now my sister thinks I'm a terrible brother.

00:46:41.454 --> 00:46:43.044
I'm a thief.

00:46:43.104 --> 00:46:45.926
I'm going to lose my good name.

00:46:45.986 --> 00:46:50.616
And I never got any money from my crime at all.

00:46:50.676 --> 00:46:56.265
Oh, what's going to happen to me?

00:46:56.325 --> 00:47:02.137
He put his head in his hands and began to cry.

00:47:02.197 --> 00:47:05.858
Holmes didn't speak for a long time.

00:47:05.918 --> 00:47:11.991
Then, in the end, he stood up and opened the door.

00:47:12.051 --> 00:47:15.242
Get out, he said.

00:47:15.302 --> 00:47:17.673
Oh, thank you.

00:47:17.733 --> 00:47:19.274
Thank you, sir.

00:47:19.334 --> 00:47:20.578
Said Ryder.

00:47:20.638 --> 00:47:25.066
Be quiet and get out," said Holmes again.

00:47:25.127 --> 00:47:34.424
And with that, Ryder ran out of the door, downstairs, out into the street, and away.

00:47:34.484 --> 00:47:38.648
After all, Watson, said Holmes.

00:47:38.708 --> 00:47:42.680
It's not my job to do the police's work for them.

00:47:42.740 --> 00:47:45.764
And young Horner's going to be all right.

00:47:45.824 --> 00:47:49.276
Ryder isn't going to go to court now.

00:47:49.336 --> 00:47:55.528
Without him, The police have no witness to say Horner was the thief.

00:47:55.588 --> 00:48:00.842
Perhaps I'm doing something wrong, but I don't believe it.

00:48:00.902 --> 00:48:05.344
I think I'm helping Ryder to be a better man.

00:48:05.404 --> 00:48:10.918
Send him to prison now, and you make him into a thief for life.

00:48:10.978 --> 00:48:13.290
But now he's afraid.

00:48:13.350 --> 00:48:16.092
He's not going to go wrong again.

00:48:16.152 --> 00:48:21.498
We found the solution to the crime And that makes me happy.

00:48:21.558 --> 00:48:23.970
And it's Christmas after all.

00:48:24.031 --> 00:48:29.217
And Christmas is a time to be nice to other people, I believe.

00:48:29.277 --> 00:48:35.805
And now Watson, let's ask Mrs. Hudson to bring in our dinner.

